#!/nix/store/pkf547jp8a92k4wmxikkyby58w10pysr-bash-interactive-5.3p9/bin/bash

# omarchy:summary=Set the Plymouth boot splash colours and logo
# omarchy:args=<background-hex> <text-hex> <path-to-logo.png>

# Upstream re-themes the boot splash to match the current Omarchy theme: it
# stages the theme with a recoloured logo, copies it into /usr/share with sudo,
# rebuilds the initramfs with mkinitcpio, and rewrites the SDDM theme's
# Main.qml on the way past.
#
# None of that can happen on NixOS, and not because anything is missing: the
# initrd is built from the configuration, so what the splash looks like is
# decided at rebuild time and cannot be edited from inside a running system.
# Left alone this produced a cascade -- a cp into a directory that does not
# exist, then mkinitcpio not found, then a tee into the store.
#
# Reachable from Style > Unlock, so it explains rather than fails. Exits
# non-zero, because the caller asked for something that did not happen.
set -euo pipefail

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
The boot splash is built with the system, not set from inside it.

  Omarchy re-themes Plymouth by writing into /usr/share and rebuilding the
  initramfs. On NixOS the initrd comes from your configuration, so the splash
  is chosen at rebuild time:

    boot.plymouth.theme = "omarchy";   # nixarchy's default
    boot.plymouth.themePackages = [ ... ];

  Nixarchy already ships Omarchy's splash and selects it. To use a different
  one, set boot.plymouth.theme yourself -- it is a mkDefault here, so your
  value wins -- and:

    sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake <your-flake>

  Everything else in Style still follows the theme at runtime. It is the boot
  splash alone that has to be decided in advance.
MSG
exit 1
